    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **53 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the tenure-based membership rule in the single place that derives `membershipLevel`: `Membership.levelOf(Owner)` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/common/Membership.java`), which the `OwnerMapper` calls to populate the DTO field.\n\n**The change:** the level formula is now `1 + email + namesake0 + tenure`, where the fourth point is granted only when tenure \u2014 days since `registrationDate` \u2014 is strictly greater than 365. Since a freshly created owner's registration date is the current business day (zero tenure), the tenure point is never earned at creation, so a new owner caps at level 3 even with an email and `namesakeCount` of 0.\n\n**Key design decision:** the spec's example mentions \"a 3-member household,\" but `Cp23Tests` pins a third household member (no email, unique first name, 3-member household) at level **2**. That proves household size is *not* a level factor \u2014 if it were, that owner would be level 3. So I left household out of the formula; it's descriptive flavor in the spec, not a contributor. This kept all four existing membership tests (`Cp15`, `Cp23`, `Cp24`) and the new `Cp39` consistent.\n\nI also handled the null-registration-date case (treated as zero tenure) and updated the class Javadoc to document the 1\u20134 range and the tenure gate.",
